WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.480
So you have to connect with
them culturally and socially.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.410
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
again, and today I want to

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.330
talk about workplace English.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.610
Uh, if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.110
the bell for notifications.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.310
And if you're following me on a
podcast service, please leave a review.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.670
So many people I know listen to
my, uh, videos, uh, or even as a

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.669
podcast to improve their English.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.435
English is, whether we like it
or not, sort of the most widely

00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:35.145
used international language.

00:00:35.745 --> 00:00:39.855
So there is a need to
learn English for work.

00:00:40.395 --> 00:00:46.305
This is particularly true of immigrants
to say Canada, the US, UK, Australia,

00:00:46.415 --> 00:00:52.905
English-speaking countries who have a need
to work in the sort of mainstream economy,

00:00:53.114 --> 00:00:57.614
therefore to use English or in the case of
Canada, French in the province of Quebec.

00:00:58.345 --> 00:00:58.944
So...

00:00:58.944 --> 00:01:05.114
and, and there are many, if we Google,
we look for, you know, professional

00:01:05.144 --> 00:01:10.965
English business, English workplace
English, there are special programs

00:01:10.965 --> 00:01:16.455
courses that are offered uh, to help
people improve their English for work.

00:01:16.995 --> 00:01:19.815
But I have some general
sort of thoughts on this.

00:01:20.505 --> 00:01:26.295
My only view is that to work in
English, the sort of technical

00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:32.415
jargon, the technical vocabulary
that you need is rather limited, uh,

00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:34.095
that can be learned quite easily.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.530
And, uh, what I would do if I were say,
having to work in German, let's say,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:47.730
or a Spanish or Chinese or Russian or
Japanese or Portuguese, then I would

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:55.440
find, you know, documents, uh, reports,
um, things of that nature, which have

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.460
the vocabulary, the technical vocabulary
that relates to my area of professional,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.050
uh, you know, expertise or things that
I need, and I would read them and I

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.890
would, if I could find podcasts on
these subjects, I would listen to them.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.200
I would save the words and
phrases that I need essentially

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.240
using something like LingQ.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.110
I would bring them into LingQ
and then work them to get that,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.790
uh, jargon, that turn of phrase,
the technical terms that I need.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:24.960
That's the easy part.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.825
The more difficult part is, because
if you work in a, an office or

00:02:31.215 --> 00:02:34.845
you're interacting with customers
or suppliers, or you're dealing with

00:02:34.845 --> 00:02:39.555
people in a working environment,
you are dealing with them socially.

00:02:39.975 --> 00:02:43.725
You're dealing with them socially while
at work, you're dealing with them in

00:02:43.725 --> 00:02:46.570
many situations socially after work.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.530
So you have to connect with
them culturally and socially,

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.120
that's more difficult.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.610
So I always say, you know, the
professional English, the business

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.000
English, that's the easy part.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.160
Work on improving your social English.

00:03:01.695 --> 00:03:08.805
And fortunately today there's an abundance
of sitcoms on YouTube on Netflix.

00:03:09.225 --> 00:03:13.275
Uh, there's different ways we
can access these to get at the,

00:03:13.535 --> 00:03:16.005
the phrasing that is being used.

00:03:16.515 --> 00:03:21.075
Uh, you know, if we can get ourselves
to a stage where we actually get the

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:26.955
jokes, um, you know, even the fact
that we watch some of the popular TV

00:03:26.955 --> 00:03:32.459
programs or see the most recent movies
gives us a subject of discussion that we

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:34.590
can share with our colleagues at work.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:39.480
Um, but also we can acquire the
vocabulary and get a sense of, of the

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:44.970
culture to the extent that these sitcoms
reflect how people actually live.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.760
It's a somewhat distorted impression.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:55.065
Nevertheless, I find when I watch a sitcom
and in...you know, Turkish or whatever.

00:03:55.065 --> 00:03:59.715
I have a feeling that I'm participating
in, in the life of these people, Turkish

00:03:59.745 --> 00:04:01.305
people, if it's a family or whatever.

00:04:01.305 --> 00:04:06.525
And I tend to prefer sitcoms with what
seems to be realistic people rather than,

00:04:07.005 --> 00:04:12.075
you know, crime and car, car races, and
shooting and all this kind of stuff.

00:04:12.465 --> 00:04:15.975
So, so we have to work even in
the workplace, we have to work

00:04:15.975 --> 00:04:21.630
on our social English as well as
our, um, professional English.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:27.030
Now, when it comes to sales
presentations or making a presentation

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:34.169
of any kind, I think it's, first
of all, you want to study people

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.849
who are effective at doing this.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.849
And the interesting thing for those
people learning English is that there

00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:41.850
is an abundance of podcasts on...

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:47.580
not only on every possible area of,
from marketing to technical issues to,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.340
uh, you name it Bitcoin, I don't know.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.110
There's, there's podcasts on everything.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.770
Uh, and there's podcasts on history
and there's podcasts on whatever

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.000
subject you're interested in.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.200
So I would definitely recommend
availing yourself of podcasts.

00:05:03.355 --> 00:05:07.135
But also, uh, I'm sure, I don't
know if there are podcasts on how to

00:05:07.135 --> 00:05:09.955
make a presentation, but there's a
lots of, I haven't Googled for it,

00:05:09.955 --> 00:05:13.315
but if you Google for how to make a
presentation, you get lots of tips.

00:05:14.395 --> 00:05:23.070
My suggestion is in any presentation I
have made, I use the sort of presentation

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:28.500
techniques of the ancient Greeks and
Romans the sort of rhetorical conventions.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.360
And you can Google rhetoric and you
will see that they have a certain style.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:34.289
You begin by...

00:05:34.679 --> 00:05:38.325
you have to begin by making
people like you in a way and

00:05:38.325 --> 00:05:40.005
establishing your credentials.

00:05:40.335 --> 00:05:43.005
And then you tell a story
and then you can present some

00:05:43.005 --> 00:05:44.685
counter-arguments to your story.

00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:47.295
And then you want to finish
with an emotional appeal.

00:05:47.415 --> 00:05:50.445
I mean, I've summarized it very
quickly, but I would definitely

00:05:50.445 --> 00:05:56.325
recommend that you do some research
into how to present things effectively.

00:05:56.475 --> 00:06:00.735
And there are a number of conflicting
theories of how to present effectively,

00:06:00.915 --> 00:06:06.090
but these are techniques that you should
have because if you have a technique

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:10.860
that you're using for how you are going
to present, how you're going to make

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.700
yourself appear credible, how you're
going to lead the audience one way and

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.360
then lead them another way and then
conclude in some other way then you have

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.130
the feeling that you are in control.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:24.240
And that is a very powerful
feeling when you are trying to

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.530
persuade people of something.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:31.050
So to summarize then, uh, by all means,
uh, and, and once we're talking about

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.900
workplace English, in most cases,
we're talking about people who are

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.284
beyond that sort of beginner level.

00:06:36.375 --> 00:06:39.675
These are people who have a certain
level in the language who need to

00:06:39.675 --> 00:06:44.055
improve that level, but beyond that want
to feel comfortable in the workplace.

00:06:44.235 --> 00:06:52.815
So my advice in summary is by all means
find technical documents, articles,

00:06:52.935 --> 00:06:58.635
material, books, podcasts that you can
access in order to get the technical

00:06:58.635 --> 00:07:01.104
jargon and phrasiology that you need.

00:07:01.875 --> 00:07:03.810
Or for that matter, if you
have, if you have to write

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.030
papers, there are forms that...

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.260
or structures that should be used
in making, you know, presenting your

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:15.150
dissertation or whatever it might be, but
then make sure you put enough effort into

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.130
the social cultural language so that you
can be comfortable with your colleagues

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.580
at work, whether at work or after work.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.050
And there are any number of ways you can
do that because nowadays there's just so

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.510
much material available on the internet.

00:07:30.539 --> 00:07:33.810
Even if you don't live in say an
English-speaking country, you can

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:40.049
access that kind of environment
through Netflix, YouTube, and other,

00:07:40.109 --> 00:07:43.770
you know, maybe presentations that
are available on, on the internet.

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:48.299
And finally, if you are in the situation
where you have to make sales presentations

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:53.130
doing a little bit of research on
effective, you know, speechmaking

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:59.340
and, and rhetoric, as I say, R H E T
O R I C, because the ancient Greeks

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.770
enrollment has really had a good approach.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.990
It was a big part of, of their education.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.170
And, uh, I think it's
very valuable even today.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:12.960
So there you have it, uh, a brief
discussion on workplace English

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:18.270
and, uh, it is part of this
whole idea of effective output.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:23.655
So I will leave a couple of videos where I
talk about using the language effectively.

